# Shadowcr3 SnapForge

**Create. Refine. Export.**

A local-first, open-source desktop photo editor. SnapForge opens a JPEG,
PNG, or WebP image, lets you crop, rotate/flip, and adjust brightness,
contrast, saturation, warmth, and sharpness with a live preview and full
undo/redo, and exports the result — without ever uploading your photo
anywhere. There is no network code in this application at all.

New to SnapForge? Install it (two commands, below), run `snapforge`, and
open **Help → Getting Started** in the app for a short walkthrough — or
just open a photo and start dragging sliders, everything can be undone.

![platform](https://img.shields.io/badge/platform-Windows%20%7C%20Linux-informational)
![license](https://img.shields.io/badge/license-GPLv3-blue)
![python](https://img.shields.io/badge/python-3.12%2B-blue)

## Why this exists

Most "simple" photo editors are either bloated (full Adobe-style suites for
a one-off crop) or cloud-based (your photos leave your machine). SnapForge
is the other option: a small, fast, offline, non-destructive editor for the
80% case — fix the crop, fix the white balance, export it — built as an
open, auditable codebase rather than a black box.

## Features (v0.1)

- **Import**: JPEG, PNG, WebP. EXIF orientation is normalized on import so
  what you see is what gets exported.
- **Adjustments**: brightness, contrast, saturation, warmth, sharpness —
  each a slider with a live preview.
- **Geometry**: crop (drag-to-select), rotate in 90° steps, flip
  horizontal/vertical.
- **Non-destructive editing**: every edit is a parameter, not a pixel
  mutation. The original is never touched in memory or on disk.
- **Full undo/redo**: every discrete action (crop, rotate, flip) is one
  undo step; dragging a slider is coalesced into exactly one step on
  release, not one step per pixel of mouse movement.
- **Before/After compare**: toggle to see the untouched original.
- **Export**: JPEG, PNG, WebP, with quality control for lossy formats.
  Exports are atomic (write-to-temp, verify, then swap in) and will never
  silently overwrite your source file.
- **Zoom**: fit-to-window by default, with zoom in/out (`Ctrl +` / `Ctrl -`),
  actual size (`Ctrl 1`), and fit (`Ctrl 0`); pans via scrollbars once
  zoomed past the window.

## Metadata policy

**Every export strips all metadata** — EXIF (including GPS coordinates,
device identifiers, capture timestamps), ICC color profile, and any
XMP/IPTC data. This is unconditional; there is no "preserve metadata"
option in v0.1. Image *orientation* specifically is not lost in practice —
it's baked into the pixel data on import, before export's stripping policy
even applies, so a photo taken in portrait stays displayed correctly.

This is a deliberate choice, not an oversight: see
[`docs/DECISIONS.md`](docs/DECISIONS.md) for why strip-everything was
chosen over selective preservation for this release.

See [`docs/ROADMAP.md`](docs/ROADMAP.md) for what's planned beyond v0.1
(curve-based white balance, RAW support, local plugin/filter system, etc.)
and [`docs/DECISIONS.md`](docs/DECISIONS.md) for the handful of scope
boundaries drawn deliberately in this release (e.g. why rotation is
quarter-turn-only for now).

