## Installation

### From source (all platforms)

Requires Python 3.12+.

```bash
git clone https://github.com/shadowcr3/Shadowcr3-SnapForge.git
cd Shadowcr3-SnapForge
python -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate        # Windows: .venv\Scripts\activate
pip install -e ".[dev]"
snapforge
```

Dependencies are pinned to exact tested versions in `pyproject.toml`, so
`pip install` fetches exactly what's needed instead of searching through
many releases — on a normal connection this should take well under a
minute. If `snapforge` isn't found after install, make sure the virtual
environment above is still active in your terminal (`source .venv/bin/
activate` again if you opened a new terminal window) — `pip install`
installs the command into that environment, not system-wide.

### Prebuilt binaries

Windows `.exe` and Linux `AppImage`/standalone builds are produced via
PyInstaller — see [`packaging/BUILD.md`](packaging/BUILD.md) for build
instructions and [`packaging/README.md`](packaging/README.md) for what a
release artifact contains. No prebuilt binaries are hosted in this
repository itself; build your own from a tagged release, or see the
project's GitHub Releases page once published.

## Usage

1. **File → Open** (or `Ctrl+O`) an image.
2. Drag the sliders in the right-hand **Adjustments** panel. The canvas
   updates live as you drag; your edit history gets exactly one entry when
   you release the slider.
3. Use the toolbar for **Rotate**, **Flip**, and **Crop** (click Crop, then
   drag a rectangle on the image).
4. Click **Before / After** to compare against the untouched original.
5. `Ctrl+Z` / `Ctrl+Y` to undo/redo. `Ctrl +`/`Ctrl -` to zoom, `Ctrl 1`
   for actual size, `Ctrl 0` to fit the window again.
6. **File → Export** (or `Ctrl+E`) to save — choose format, quality (for
   JPEG/WebP), and destination. SnapForge will ask before overwriting an
   existing file and will never overwrite your original without that
   explicit confirmation.

**Help → Getting Started** in the app has this same walkthrough, and
**Help → About SnapForge** shows the version you're running.

## Architecture

```
src/snapforge/
├── imaging/        Pure image operations (no Qt, no filesystem). The only
│                    place pixel math happens. Fully unit-testable.
├── models/          ImageDocument (protected source + edit params) and
│                    EditHistory (undo/redo over immutable EditParams
│                    snapshots — not image buffers).
├── services/        Import/export: content-sniffed decode, atomic
│                    temp-file-then-replace writes, overwrite protection.
├── controllers/      EditorController — the only thing the UI talks to for
│                    editing logic. Owns no widgets, does no painting.
├── ui/              PySide6 widgets. Presentation only — never calls
│                    imaging/ or services/ directly.
└── main.py          Entry point: stand up QApplication + MainWindow.
```

The one invariant everything else depends on: **`imaging.operations.render()`
is the single code path both the live preview and the final export use.**
Preview and export can never silently disagree, because they're not two
implementations — they're one function called with and without a
downscale step. See the docstring on `render()` for the canonical,
explicitly-ordered operation pipeline (rotate/flip → crop → color →
sharpen) and why that order was chosen.

## Verifying a build

`snapforge --self-test` runs a scripted open → adjust → rotate/flip → crop
→ undo/redo → zoom → export sequence with no window shown and prints
`PASS`/`FAIL`. Useful for confirming a packaged binary actually works, not
just that it was built without error — see `packaging/BUILD.md`.

## Testing

```bash
pip install -e ".[dev]"
pytest                      # 95 tests: imaging, history, document, import/export,
                             # controller (pytest-qt), canvas coordinate mapping
ruff check src/ tests/      # lint
```

`imaging/`, `models/`, and `services/` have no Qt dependency and are tested
as plain Python. `controllers/` and `ui/` are tested with `pytest-qt`
against a real (headless/offscreen) `QApplication`, driving actual Qt
signals rather than mocking them — see `tests/test_controller.py` and
`tests/test_canvas.py`.

Test fixtures are procedurally generated at test time (see
`tests/conftest.py`); no personal photographs are shipped as test data.

## Contributing

See [`CONTRIBUTING.md`](CONTRIBUTING.md). Short version: open an issue
before a large PR, keep `imaging/` free of Qt and filesystem code, run
`pytest` and `ruff check` before submitting.

